WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
Thread lifetime is a core part of secure concurrent design.

2
00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:09.393
A thread should have a clear owner, a clear unit of work, and a clear shutdown path.

3
00:00:09.393 --> 00:00:16.003
If the code cannot explain who is responsible for stopping and joining a thread, cleanup risk is already present.

4
00:00:16.003 --> 00:00:20.525
A std::thread must be joined or otherwise handled according to its contract.

5
00:00:20.525 --> 00:00:28.179
Joining gives the owning code a synchronization point and helps ensure that referenced objects are still alive while the thread uses them.

6
00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:34.093
std::jthread, when available, improves this pattern by joining in its destructor and supporting cooperative stop requests.

7
00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:35.832
Detached threads are review-sensitive.

8
00:00:35.832 --> 00:00:42.442
Detaching removes the easy join point and makes lifetime, shutdown, error reporting, and resource cleanup harder to reason about.

9
00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:48.704
It may be appropriate in narrow designs, but it should not be the default escape from ownership complexity.

10
00:00:48.704 --> 00:00:51.835
Captured this is a common source of lifetime risk.

11
00:00:51.835 --> 00:01:00.532
A background task that stores a pointer or reference to an object must not run after that object is destroyed unless ownership has been transferred

12
00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:02.272
or lifetime is intentionally extended.

13
00:01:02.272 --> 00:01:04.359
Cancellation should be cooperative and explicit.

14
00:01:04.359 --> 00:01:10.273
Threads should check stop flags, stop tokens, queue shutdown states, or other documented signals at appropriate points.

15
00:01:10.273 --> 00:01:14.100
Forced assumptions about when a thread will stop make cleanup brittle.

16
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.231
Thread pools and background workers need the same discipline.

17
00:01:17.231 --> 00:01:25.232
Work items should not outlive the data they reference, and shutdown should stop accepting new work, notify waiting workers, drain or cancel pending

18
00:01:25.232 --> 00:01:28.015
work according to policy, and join worker threads.

19
00:01:28.015 --> 00:01:33.234
Review lifetime by following references, captures, buffers, callbacks, and ownership transfers across the thread boundary.

20
00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:38.800
Secure cleanup depends on knowing which objects remain valid until each thread is done with them.
